<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxyrhynchus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oxyrhynchus, modern el-Bahnasa, was a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the capital of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> in Ptolemaic and Roman times. Excavations by Bernard Grenfell and Arthur Hunt from 1896 recovered hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> fragments from its rubbish mounds. They include tax records, letters and contracts as well as lost works of Greek literature and early Christian texts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus.jpg" alt="Oxyrhynchus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdallah ali · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B7%D9%82%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D9%87%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%87%D9%86%D8%B3%D8%A7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of papyri, above all from <b>Oxyrhynchus</b>, the Faiyum villages and Karanis, record leases, taxes, lawsuits, letters and school exercises.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Much of this literature survives because Byzantine scholars copied it for centuries; papyri found in Egypt since the 19th century, above all at <b>Oxyrhynchus</b>, have restored works thought lost, such as plays of Menander and poems of Sappho.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Papyri such as Papyrus <b>Oxyrhynchus</b> 20 and 21 preserve parts of the text, and in May 2026 a fragment of the Iliad was found placed inside an Egyptian mummy of around 400 CE, the first such text known from a funerary context.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>It was found in 1918 at <b>Oxyrhynchus</b> in Egypt and published in 1922, and was kept in the Papyrology Rooms of the Sackler Library in Oxford, renamed the Bodleian Art, Archaeology and Ancient World Library in 2022.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oxyrhynchus-hymn">Oxyrhynchus hymn</a></li></ul></section>
